A||||3||ponya||Rear Main Seal||||1||
Z||000000||ponya||06-26-2000||05:57 AM||||This weekend, my son and I changed the muffler system on my 86SE. While the muffler was down, we changed the gasket on the oil pan which had oil dripping from it for some time. Both those jobs went well. After taking the car for a ride, we let it sit and a while later we checked under the car and there were a couple of small drips. My son thinks its the rear main seal. Can this be changed in my garage or is this a job for Pontiac? Any suggestions are appreciated. Thanks Bob||||reg||1
Z||000001||GWMORGAN||06-26-2000||10:36 AM||||If your car is a stick shift, you'll have to remove the tranny, pressure plate, clutch and fly wheel. There you'll find the rear main oil seal. If you know what you're doing and have done it before, it should'nt be that hard with someone helping you. On the other hand, it's a tough a** job if you have'nt a clue.||||reg||1
Z||000002||bHooper||06-26-2000||11:53 AM||||i replaced mine, when i put a new tranny in.  i had never done this before, it seemed rather easy, however, i have noticed a small "weap" from that area.  i haven't checked this in several weeks, so i need to look again.<P>i would recomend that you purchase a seal from GM... they look more durable than the NAPA seal i used.  the difficult part is "setting" the seal.  i believe that NAPA or GM offers a tool for this... i used my own judgement, which is somewhat suspect  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||06-26-2000||01:19 PM||||I'm not 100% sure this holds for Fiero, but remove the shield from the tranny and see if there is oil in the bell housing. Most times there will be oil in the bell housing if the rear main is gone, but not if it's the oil pan. ((You may have failed to get a good seal at one of the joints in the new pan gasket.)) On most cars the shield extends far enough up to prevent oil from a pan gasket getting inside the bell.<P>the tranny comes out or the motor does, or however you feel like is easiest for you. taking out the tranny is probly a tad easier. It's a nasty job no matter how you do it.<P>GM lists a special tool for the seal. It might be available for rent/signout else where.<P>This is definatly one place I think I'd use an OE GM part.<P>Obviously you don't want to knick the crank taking out the old seal, but you also must make sure you don't scratch the walls of the "hole" taking out the old seal or the new seal will leak around the out side. ((This type of leak may not throw oil in the bell housing. It depends on where around the seal the leak is and wether the oil gets on the crank or not.)) If there are any scratches in the walls of the hole, paint the walls with Permatex "Super 300" sealer or Balkamp "Aviation" sealer before setting the new seal. (These sealers will not make boogers like RTV silicone will. RTV doesn't work well for this.) If you can't tell for cetain the hole is perfect then paint it with sealer. [this works for all seals of this type, like axle seals.]<P>The main puting it in thing is if you can't get the tool to press it in that you don't bend it. Find someting the same diameter as the seal and a put a block of wood over it and tap it in that way. try to drive it as straight as posible.||||reg||1
